A generic type parameter preserves a relationship between inputs and outputs. An identity function using `T` returns the same type it receives; an `any` version accepts everything but loses that relationship and permits unchecked operations. TypeScript often infers type arguments from call-site values, while explicit type arguments remain available when inference is insufficient. Add constraints such as `T extends { length: number }` when the implementation needs a capability shared by the permitted types.

```ts caption="The generic preserves the element type without `any`."
function first<T>(items: readonly T[]): T | undefined {
  return items[0];
}
const n = first([1, 2]); // number | undefined
```

`keyof T` produces a union of keys known for `T`. Constraining a second type parameter with `K extends keyof T` links the key argument to the object argument, so an invalid property name is rejected and the return type can be `T[K]`. This is more precise than accepting `string` and returning `unknown` or `any`. The constraint expresses a relationship, while indexed access retrieves the corresponding value type.

```ts caption="The key and result types stay connected."
function get<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}
const value = get({ id: 1, name: 'Ada' }, 'name'); // string
```

Give each object variant a common property with distinct literal values, such as `status: 'loading' | 'success' | 'error'`. A `switch` or equality check on that discriminant narrows the object to the matching variant, so variant-specific fields become available and invalid combinations are rejected. Add an exhaustive fallback that accepts `never` when the state space must be updated whenever a new variant is introduced. This models states explicitly instead of relying on several loosely related optional fields.

```ts caption="The literal discriminant narrows each variant."
type Result =
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'success'; data: string }
  | { status: 'error'; message: string };
function text(r: Result) {
  switch (r.status) {
    case 'success': return r.data;
    case 'error': return r.message;
    case 'loading': return 'Loading';
  }
}
```
